<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhojshala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bhoj Shala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhojshala is a monument in Dhar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, associated by tradition with the 11th-century Paramara king Bhoja, a famous patron of learning, and held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> to have been a temple of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> study. Muslims regard it as the Kamal Maula Mosque. Protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a>, it has long been the subject of a religious dispute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhojshala.jpg" alt="Bhojshala" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhojshala_Yagna_pit_in_Courtyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition links the complex to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhoja-paramara">Raja Bhoja</a>, who ruled in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 1000 to 1055 and to whom many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works on poetics, grammar, astronomy and architecture are attributed. According to some historians the building itself dates mainly from the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, while the Islamic domed tombs of the wider campus were added in the fourteenth and fifteenth. The Pawar rulers of the Dhar Maratha State repaired it in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> did so more extensively after 1947.</p><p>The name Bhojshala was attached to the building only in 1903, by K. K. Lele, Superintendent of Education in Dhar State. Writers of the 1900s called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhoja-paramara">Raja Bhoja</a>'s school, and by the 1930s rulings of Dhar State used the term Bhojshala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1903 Lele reported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> inscriptions in the walls and floor of the pillared hall. Among them are the Kūrmaśataka, verses in praise of the primal tortoise attributed to Bhoja but engraved probably under Arjunavarman in the 13th century, and part of the drama Vijayaśrīnāṭikā by Madana, which says it was performed before Arjunavarman in the temple of Sarasvatī. Two serpentine grammatical charts date from the time of Naravarman, who ruled about 1094 to 1133. John Malcolm had earlier removed an inscribed panel, the Rāüla vela of Roḍa, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>.</p><p>A sculpture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, published in 1924 as Bhoja's Sarasvatī from Dhar, was found in 1875 at the site of the city palace. Its inscription, as later read by Harivallabh Bhayani, records the making of an image of the Jain goddess Ambikā.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Jains and Muslims have all claimed the site. Under rules of 2003, Hindus and Jains could pray there every Tuesday and Muslims every Friday. After a writ of March 2024, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> surveyed the complex for 98 days, recovering over 1,700 relics and concluding that the structure incorporates parts of an earlier Paramara complex. On 15 May 2026 the Indore bench of the Madhya Pradesh High Court declared it a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> and cancelled the 2003 order allowing Muslims to offer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khutba">Friday prayers</a> there. Some Jain experts criticised the verdict, holding that the idol is of the Jain goddess Ambika.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhojshala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Maharaja governed a mainly Hindu population; the <b>Bhoj Shala</b> and the Dhar fort are its main monuments.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhar-state">Dhar State</a></li></ul></section>
